86176135 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176135 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176135 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186176135 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "275b0afa-011e-599e-83db-08f12becae8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176335 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176335 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176335 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176335 #edb/ref 1071 "| **Age, in years ($x$)** | $6$ | $10$ | $12$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Education, in years ($y$)** | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n\nThe table above shows the ages and corresponding years of formal education completed by each of Mark's cousins.\nCompute the covariance of the data sets given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=11$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176335 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "41c31e68-b6d7-5283-bb82-6cc7ac671a08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176335 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176335 #edb/ref 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(6-11)(2-6)+(10-11)(4-6)+(12-11)(8-6)+(16-11)(10-6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-5)(-4)+(-1)(-2)+(1)(2)+(5)(4)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[20+2+2+20] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 44 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176655 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176655 #edb/ref 1061 217080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176655 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a9620c91-7f1f-5c90-81f9-15b63580b9b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176655 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176656 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176656 #edb/ref 1061 217081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176656 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7c94f745-4162-5e7d-841a-0a7d3f00e84a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176656 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176657 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176657 #edb/ref 1061 217084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176657 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "48e20fe8-234e-5200-b8a8-908f518d0da4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176657 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176658 #edb/ref 1061 217087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176658 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2661268b-e076-5805-af10-02ffd1c3fc5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176658 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176950 #edb/ref 1064 "Given two finite data sets of equal length, say,\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}, \\qquad \ny=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\},\n$$\n\nthe **covariance** between $x$ and $y,$ denoted by $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y),$ is a measure that evaluates the extent to which $x$ and $y$ change together.\n\n- A positive covariance means that if $x$ increases, then $y$ is also likely to increase:\n\n![](images/bb/bbfae3f0fb38bc8652cd26716e4b0b128cf1f2039350267034d97161d7f5dac4.png)\n\n- A negative covariance means that if $x$ increases, then $y$ is likely to decrease:\n\n![](images/fd/fd45053fdc39f070e3438c3b8c61fe01613201595cfe00bd4cc6c6e481bf73ab.png)\n\n- A covariance of zero (or close to zero) indicates that there is no *linear* relationship between $x$ and $y.$\n\n![](images/a5/a58fa4d40d77af23df86b5bd8eaca3119b93bc9ea97cc435eb4e53ba2777b071.png)\n\n \n\n**Watch Out!** A covariance of zero does *not* mean that $x$ and $y$ are not related. It simply means the relationship between them is not linear. To illustrate, consider the image below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4bdbf92e691174644dda9b9a84acb1b896c0436afb221501dc10b4635841539.png)\n\n \n\nThe (nonlinear) relationship shown above is $y=x^2.$ However, it can be shown that $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) = 0$ for this data set.\n\nTo calculate $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y),$ we use the following formula: \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$  and $\\overline{y}$ denote the means of $x$ and $y$ respectively.\n\n**Note**: This formula for the covariance assumes that $x$ and $y$ are drawn from a *population*. There is a slightly different formula for covariance when $x$ and $y$ are sample data. However, we won't worry about the difference for now." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176950 #edb/ref 1065 10301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176950 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "da03b3e7-b452-59b9-b81e-5dd75cea02a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176950 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176951 #edb/ref 1064 "Variance measures the spread of a *single* data set $x$ around its mean value $\\overline{x}.$ On the other hand, covariance measures the relationship between *two* data sets $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe (population) covariance of the data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}, \\qquad  \ny=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nis given by \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y}).\n$$\n\nIf we compare a single data set, say $x$, against itself using our formula for covariance, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,x) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\sigma^2$ denotes the variance of $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176951 #edb/ref 1065 10305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176951 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "42b9ca98-be0b-50e0-9b90-f3e38732fcd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176951 #edb/ref 1067 "The Connection Between Variance and Covariance" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266576 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266576 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266576 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "311e284a-a84b-5e7f-b5a1-e35c8f0d5168" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267036 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267036 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267036 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "64ccf930-ce1a-5735-bf79-470826f036dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267185 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267185 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267185 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "76132fbf-5e71-5e70-90ec-a505fc2186fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267317 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267317 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267317 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "853262fc-b92a-5438-baec-a53e6c209102" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267611 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267611 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267611 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a31cd77b-6b2c-52dc-a626-7500d351a1a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268716 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268716 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268716 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268716 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268716 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268716 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268716 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268716 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186266576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268716 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "aca651ef-8c84-5b8a-8875-73fe007986c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268969 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268969 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134155" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268969 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268969 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{4,\\,6,\\,8,\\,2\\},\\,y=\\{2,\\,4,\\,10,\\,8\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=5$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=6.$ Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268969 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1d0e8ef1-dbd3-5d5d-b9cb-f3092550c8dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268969 #edb/ref 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4-5)(2-6)+(6-5)(4-6)+(8-5)(10-6)+(2-5)(8-6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-1)(-4)+(1)(-2)+(3)(4)+(-3)(2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[4-2+12-6] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272571 #edb/ref 1080 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272571 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272571 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1499dc15-38f5-5d95-918a-72ffb6809888" #edb/ref 1