59 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535993 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134213" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535993 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535993 #edb/ref 1071 "| **Price per Bag, in $$$ ($x$)** | $2$ | $3$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Number of Bags Sold ($y$)** | $28$ | $20$ | $24$ | $16$ |\n\nThe table above shows the results of a survey conducted in four fast food outlets. It shows data on the number of bags of onion rings sold daily and the price per bag. Calculate the covariance of the data sets." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535993 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cd62ff9e-af99-5108-83c4-43b98934fd5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535993 #edb/ref 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (2+3+3+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (28+20+24+16) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 88 \\\\  & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(2\\,-\\,3)(28\\,-\\,22)+(3\\,-\\,3)(20\\,-\\,22)+(3\\,-\\,3)(24\\,-\\,22)+(4\\,-\\,3)(16\\,-\\,22)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-1)(6)+(0)(-2)+(0)(2)+(1)(-6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[-6+0+0+(-6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535999 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186535866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535999 #edb/ref 1069 "q-149613" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535999 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535999 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{2,\\,14,\\,18,\\,6\\},\\,y=\\{-2,\\,-1,\\,-3,\\,2\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=10$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=-1.$ Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535999 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dbf7f381-31f1-5a31-a806-20d912ae1de5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535999 #edb/ref 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(2-10)(-2-(-1))+(14-10)(-1-(-1)) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,+(18-10)(-3-(-1))+(6-10)(2-(-1))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-8)(-1)+(4)(0)+(8)(-2)+(-4)(3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[8+0-16-12] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-20) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
